L000351	\section{Notations and conventions}
L000352	The lattice constant $a$ is set to unity, unless stated otherwise. We use $E$ and $E_{\rm F}$ to denote the reference value of the energy and the Fermi energy, respectively. The quantity $\omega$ bears a meaning of the energy difference, hybridization energy, or the frequency of the applied ac electric field expressed in units of energy, depending on the context. With $d(E)$ we denote the characteristic spatial extent of states at energy $E$. We use the notation $\llangle\ldots\rrangle$ for the disorder average, reserving $\braket{\ldots}$ for matrix elements of operators. For the numerical simulations performed for the SSH chain with chiral disorder, we fix the sum of the inequivalent hoppings to a constant value $t+t' = 2$: the remaining free parameters of the model are therefore the difference $t-t'$ and the disorder strength $W$. The edge states are removed from the spectrum if present.
L000353	
L000354	\section{Wavefunctional decay of zero modes in the SSH chain with chiral disorder}
L000355	\label{appa}
L000356	
L000357	In this section, we review the properties of the zero-energy states in the SSH chain with chiral disorder, showing that they decay exponentially at long distances when the system is away from criticality, and stretched-exponentially (as $\psi \sim e^{-s \sqrt{x}}$) at the critical point.
L000358	
L000359	We consider the Schrodinger equation $\hat H_{\rm SSH} \ket{\psi} = 0$ with the Hamiltonian
L000360	\begin{equation}
L000361	    \hat H_{\rm SSH} = \sum_i t_i' \ket{i,\rm B} \bra{i,\rm A}  + t_i \ket{i+1,\rm B} \bra{i,\rm A} + {\rm h.c.} \, .
L000362	    \label{sshhda}
L000363	\end{equation}
L000364	Using the real-space representation
L000365	$\ket{\psi} = \sum_i \left( \psi_{i \rm A} \ket{i, \rm A} + \psi_{i \rm B} \ket{i, \rm B} \right)$, we obtain
L000366	\begin{equation}
L000367	\begin{aligned}
L000368	    t_i' \psi_{i\rm A} + t_i \psi_{i-1\rm A} & = 0 \, , \\
L000369	    t_i' \psi_{i\rm B} + t_i \psi_{i+1\rm B} & = 0 \, .
L000370	\end{aligned}
L000371	\end{equation}
L000372	By iterating the above expressions, the growth(decay) rates of the zero modes localized on A and B sub-lattices are found
L000373	\begin{equation}
L000374	    \xi_{0\rm B}^{-1} = -\xi_{0\rm A}^{-1} = \frac{1}{N} \log \left| \psi_{N\rm A}/\psi_{1\rm A} \right|  = \frac{1}{N} \sum_i^N \log \left|   t_i'/t_i\right| \, .
L000375	    \label{xiavg}
L000376	\end{equation}
L000377	Since the sum of the random quantities on the right-hand side is self-averaging, we can introduce the typical localization length of the mode localized on the B sub-lattice as defined by the law of large numbers
L000378	\begin{equation}
L000379	\xi_{0}^{-1} = \left \llangle \log \left|   t'_i /t_i\right| \right \rrangle \, .
L000380	\end{equation}
L000381	If the expression on the right-hand side is finite, the zero mode decays exponentially on average. If, on the other hand, the right-hand side vanishes, the exponent of the wavefunction, as we can see from \eqref{xiavg}, undergoes a random walk with zero mean. The spatial decay is stretched-exponential, and characterized by a length scale $s$:
L000382	\begin{equation}
L000383	    \left\llangle |\log \psi_N|^2 \right\rrangle - \left\llangle |\log \psi_N| \right\rrangle^2 = \sqrt{\left\llangle \left( \sum_{i=1}^N \log \left| t_i'/t_i \right| \right)^2 \right\rrangle} = s \sqrt{N} \, , \qquad s = \llangle \log^2|t_i'/t_i| \rrangle - \llangle \log|t_i'/t_i| \rrangle^2 \, .
L000384	\end{equation}
L000385	Therefore, we recover the average stretched-exponential zero mode behavior $\psi(x) \sim e^{-s \sqrt{x}}$. The qualifier \textit{average} is extremely important here, as the critical wavefunctions would typically exhibit multiple minima and maxima due to the fluctuations in the winding number. As we are primarily interested in disorder-averaged low-frequency transport properties, the leading stretched-exponential part of the wavefunction profile is sufficient.
L000386	
L000387	\section{Mott resonances in the bond-diluted SSH chain}
L000388	\label{appb}
L000389	
L000390	In this section, we derive the hybridization energy of the zero modes analytically in the bond-diluted SSH chain model. We, therefore, consider \eqref{sshhda}, with the hoppings missing with a probability $p$ from the unperturbed chain with parameters $t$, $t'$.
L000391	
L000392	\begin{figure}[t!]
L000393	        \centering
L000394	        \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{appplot1}
L000395	    \caption{$a)$ Two zero modes ($\psi_{\rm L}$ and $\psi_{\rm R}$) appear on both sides of the topological region created by the bond-dilution disorder in the SSH chain. $b)$ If the segment is sufficiently long, the zero modes hybridize into sub-gap bonding, and anti-bonding states $\psi_{\pm}$. Due to the finite gap, only the optical transitions between such states (indicated with a purple arrow) will contribute to the low-frequency ac conductivity.}
L000396	    \label{appplot1}
L000397	\end{figure}
L000398	
L000399	As argued in the main text, at low frequency, only the transitions between the hybridized topological zero modes shown in \autoref{appplot1} are relevant for transport. We therefore consider a topological segment of the length $d$, as shown in the image, assuming $|t'|<|t|$. The normalized unperturbed zero modes localized on the ends of the chain take the form
L000400	\begin{equation}
L000401	\begin{aligned}
L000402	\ket{\psi_{\rm L}} & = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\xi_0}} \sum_{0 \leq i \leq d} e^{-i/\xi_0} \ket{i, \rm A} \, , \\
L000403	\ket{\psi_{\rm R}} & = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\xi_0}}  \sum_{0 \leq i \leq d} e^{(i-d)/\xi_0} \ket{i, \rm B} \, ,
L000404	\end{aligned}
L000405	\label{psiab}
L000406	\end{equation}
L000407	where $\xi_0 = \log^{-1}|t/t'|$, and $d, \xi_0$ assumed large. By repeatedly using the decimation procedure outlined in the next Appendix, the Hamiltonian of the segment is reduced to
L000408	\begin{equation}
L000409	\hat H_{0} = t' (t'/t)^d \hat c^\dagger_{0\rm A} \hat c_{d\rm B} + {\rm h.c.} \, .
L000410	\end{equation}
L000411	Note that the parameter $(t'/t)^d$ is always small for sufficiently long sections, which justifies the application of the perturbation theory to obtain the low-lying in energy states. Using degenerate perturbation theory, the energies of the hybridized states $\ket{\psi_{\pm}}$ are found as
L000412	\begin{equation}
L000413	E_\pm \simeq \pm \braket{\psi_{\rm L}| \hat H_{0} | \psi_{\rm R}} = \pm 2t'/\xi_0 (t'/t)^d =\pm 2t'/\xi_0 e^{-d/\xi_0} \, .
L000414	\end{equation}
L000415	Therefore, in the bond-diluted SSH chain, the energy of the low-lying Mott resonances depends exponentially on their size $d$.
